(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2609075800-register-cloud-itonami-cli")
  (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-cli is registered, and the other three repositories are not ready yet")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-07")
  (:adr/body "## What was asked

itonami.cloud の repo を cloud-itonami-app / -cli / -api / -apex に整理し、
-auth も分離する（オーナー、2026-09-07）。

`cloud-itonami-cli` is the first of the four, and this registers it.

## Why this one first, and why not the others yet

The seam was measured before anything moved, and measured twice, because the
first measurement asked the wrong half of the question.

The first pass recorded that `-cli` was clean because `commands.cljc` and
`repo_profile.cljc` require nothing from the app. True — and it is the other
direction that decides whether they can move. Who requires THEM:

    cloud.itonami.app.repo-profile   bin/itonami, its own test. No app-side use.
    cloud.itonami.app.commands       bin/itonami, tests, and cli.clj.

That one app-side consumer sets the dependency direction between two of the
four repositories: if `cli.clj` is live, moving `commands` makes the app depend
on the CLI, and leaving it makes the CLI depend on an app checkout at runtime —
the coupling the split exists to remove.

`cli.clj` is neither. There is no `:cli` alias in the app's `deps.edn`; the
file's own comment says a test goes red \"the day :cli is reintroduced\". It is
tested but has no launcher — PR #270 removed it and `bin/itonami` replaced it.
So there was no design fork, only a measurement not yet taken.

The other three are not ready in the same sense:

- **`-apex`** is separable today — five Cloudflare Workers (`agent-edge`,
  `app-edge`, `itonami-cloud-webhooks`, `itonami-fleet-dispatch`, `mcp-edge`)
  with their own wrangler configs. Next.
- **`-auth`** needs a decision first: `identity.clj` is 3,645 lines and
  `server.clj` calls `require-session!` / `require-app-session!` /
  `require-human-session!` from route bodies throughout. Splitting means
  deciding what the app asks the auth library FOR — a session record, or a
  decision. That is a design question, not a move.
- **`-api`** needs a refactor before a repository helps: `server.clj` is 7,425
  lines of routes AND handlers in one file. There is no HTTP layer to lift away
  from a domain layer yet, and that refactor can happen inside the current repo.

## What this commit does

Registers `orgs/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-cli` in `repos.edn`
`:extra-projects` and adds the single west entry at pin `cb85ffd3`, generated
with `--entry` so the diff is five lines. Server-side pin verification ran:
new entry, reachable from main.

## Nothing was deleted from the app

Both trees carry the CLI today, both suites pass, and `~/.local/bin/itonami`
still resolves through `~/.cloud-itonami/current`. Removing the app's copy
means repointing that launcher, so it is a separate change and there is never a
moment when nothing works.

The hazard a split like this creates is not the move — it is the three
resources the CLI now pins (`commands.edn`, `cli-aliases.edn`,
`defaults.edn`), which the app *generates* from its own route table. A pinned
copy of a generated file drifts. `scripts/verify-pinned-surface.cljs` in the
CLI repo answers in three outcomes rather than two: 0 identical, 1 drift naming
the file and both digests, 2 REFUSED when no app checkout was found — because
nothing compared is not a pass.

Its first version did not discriminate: it took the first candidate path that
existed, so `--app /nonexistent` fell through to the sibling default and
returned 0. The control written to prove REFUSED works is what caught it.")))
